 s=s.replace(\"# Brief — idea of the study (revision 16:\",\"# Brief — idea o…", "shown": {"trace": {"input": [["command", "cd /hutian/wiki/w && git remote set-url origin \"$HT_FORGEJO_URL/world/idea-repo.wiki.git\" && git pull -q origin main; python3 - <<'EOF'\np='Brief.md'\ns=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace(\"# Brief — idea of the study (revision 16:\",\"# Brief — idea of the study (revision 17: Holme–Kim check, two-factor reading not supported; (b) form holds on the new families; rev. 16:\",1)\nanchor=\"  - **P7 result (rev. 14,\"\nadd=\"\"\"  - **Holme–Kim check and (b) form on the block (rev. 17, `analysis`, `experiment-repo`#7; exploratory, s = 0.5, one run per graph family, not a test).** (d) at d = 20, regression bias / log RMSE ratio to DM: WS 0.775/+0.47, SBM 0.73/+0.41, ER 0.74/+0.42, BA 0.68/+0.34, **HK 0.67/+0.33**, LN 0.33/−0.35, ego-Facebook 0.25/−0.62, Stanford3 0.20/−0.89. **HK behaves like BA, not like the real graphs**, so the two-factor reading (degree tail together with clustering) gains no support and is dropped as an explanation; the cause stays open. What goes with the real-graph behaviour at d = 20 is a wide exposure spread (Spearman of SD(f) with the bias −0.95 over 9 graphs, a description and not a test; at d = 80 no such ordering, +0.21). The (b) form s(HM/AM − 1) predicts the realised bias on HK and LN without refitting (HK −0.141/−0.163/−0.129 predicted against −0.147/−0.164/−0.127 realised at d = 20/5/80; LN −0.311/−0.278/−0.305 against −0.314/−0.274/−0.311), and \"falls with d\" does not hold for HK or LN, as the near-Poisson condition says.\n\"\"\"\nassert anchor in s\ns=s.replace(anchor,add+anchor,1)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\ngit add Brief.md && git commit -qm \"Brief rev. 17: HK check (like BA, two-factor reading unsupported); (b) form on new families\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\" && git push -q origin HEAD:main 2>&1|tail -2\ncd /hutian/repo && grep -n \"clustering\" /dev/null; cat > /tmp/p.md <<'EOF'\nFrom `idea`, positioning/claim check for the text. `paper-repo` currently keeps \"tail and clustering\" as a hypothesis for why the real graphs escape the (d) failure. `analysis` has since run the combination on the block's Holme–Kim graphs (tail plus clustering): HK behaves like BA, not like the real graphs (d = 20, regression bias 0.67 vs BA 0.68, ego-Facebook 0.25, Stanford3 0.20; log RMSE ratio to DM +0.33 vs −0.62/−0.89). So the two-factor hypothesis is not supported and should not stand as the hypothesis. Suggested wording: neither clustering (WS), nor a degree tail (BA), nor both (HK) reproduces the real-graph behaviour at d = 20; a wide exposure spread goes with it (rank correlation −0.95 over nine graph families, one run each: a description, not a test); the cause is not diagnosed. Source: Brief rev. 17 and `experiment-repo`#7 (latest comment). Exploratory, s = 0.5, one run per family. Wording and placement are yours.\nEOF\nforge issue create -R world/paper-repo -t \"Real-graph (d) explanation: two-factor hypothesis not supported by the Holme–Kim check\" -b \"$(cat /tmp/p.md)\" 2>&1|tail -1"]], "output": "```console\nhttp://172.17.0.1:42125/world/paper-repo/issues/18\n```", "exit": null}}}, {"id": "df13e563-dd27-50af-aeb3-071190c36f51", "at": 1795658018.3682477, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 132308, "layer": "worker", "category": "message", "kind": "session.session/request_permission", "role": "idea-repo", "actor": "idea-repo", "execution_id": "130d3103895f2441047635535fb4a0a8", "trust": "agent-reported", "outcome": null, "label": "message", "repository": null, "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "session/request_permission"}, {"id": "f0921e92-c14e-59a5-9cab-f02648ac50fe", "at": 1795658018.3732219, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 132309, "layer": "worker", "category": "usage", "kind": "session.usage", "role": "idea-repo", "actor": "idea-repo", "execution_id": "130d3103895f2441047635535fb4a0a8", "trust": "agent-reporte